.TH tuzipfs n 0.2 tclutils
.SH tclutils::tuzipfs
.PP
\fBtclutils::tuzipfs\fR is a small Tcl 9 \fBzipfs\fR convenience wrapper. The package loads under Tcl 8.6 as well, but commands that need \fBzipfs\fR throw a clear \fBTCLUTILS TUZIPFS UNAVAILABLE\fR error.
.PP
Since 0.2 it also provides a handful of \fBimage primitives\fR for assembling self\-contained Tcl 9 applications (zipkits) with \fBzipfs mkimg\fR.
.SS Commands
.CS
package require tclutils::tuzipfs

::tclutils::tuzipfs::available
::tclutils::tuzipfs::requireAvailable
::tclutils::tuzipfs::root
::tclutils::tuzipfs::mounts
::tclutils::tuzipfs::mount archive.zip ?mountName?
::tclutils::tuzipfs::unmount mountpoint
::tclutils::tuzipfs::listFiles mountpoint ?-glob pattern? ?-recursive boolean?
::tclutils::tuzipfs::find mountpoint ?options?
::tclutils::tuzipfs::exists zipfsPath
::tclutils::tuzipfs::readFile zipfsPath
::tclutils::tuzipfs::withMounted archive.zip mountVar body ?-mount mountName?
.CE
.SS "Image primitives (0.2)"
.CS
::tclutils::tuzipfs::rcopy src dst
::tclutils::tuzipfs::copyStdlib destDir ?-tk boolean? ?-from running|DIR?
::tclutils::tuzipfs::mkimg outfile indir ?-strip DIR? ?-basekit FILE? ?-password PW?
::tclutils::tuzipfs::buildImage -out FILE -vfs DIR ?-basekit FILE? ?-stdlib none|cli|gui? ?-stdlibfrom running|DIR?
.CE
.IP \(bu
\fBrcopy\fR — recursive byte\-exact copy. Works from a mounted \fBzipfs\fR source, where a plain \fBfile copy\fR is unreliable.
.IP \(bu
\fBcopyStdlib\fR — copy \fBtcl_library\fR (and, with \fB\-tk 1\fR, \fBtk_library\fR) into \fBdestDir\fR. \fB\-from running\fR uses the current interpreter's \fB[info library]\fR / \fB$::tk_library\fR; \fB\-from DIR\fR reads them from \fBDIR/tcl_library\fR (and \fBDIR/tk_library\fR) — point this at a foreign basekit's mountpoint for cross\-platform builds.
.IP \(bu
\fBmkimg\fR — checked wrapper over \fBzipfs mkimg\fR. Files in \fBindir\fR land at the image's archive root because \fB\-strip\fR defaults to \fBindir\fR. \fB\-basekit\fR is the template interpreter (default: the running executable, which must itself be a static zipkit).
.IP \(bu
\fBbuildImage\fR — one\-call convenience: optionally drop the stdlib into an already\-assembled VFS tree (\fB\-stdlib cli|gui\fR), then \fBmkimg\fR.
.SS "Example — read from an archive"
.CS
tcl::tm::path add lib/tm
package require tclutils::tuzipfs

if {[::tclutils::tuzipfs::available]} {
    ::tclutils::tuzipfs::withMounted document.odt mp {
        puts [::tclutils::tuzipfs::listFiles $mp -glob *.xml]
        puts [::tclutils::tuzipfs::readFile [file join $mp content.xml]]
    }
}
.CE
.SS "Example — build a standalone application"
.CS
package require tclutils::tuzipfs
namespace import ::tclutils::tuzipfs::*

# Assemble a VFS tree: the app as main.tcl at the archive root.
file mkdir app.vfs
file copy myapp.tcl [file join app.vfs main.tcl]

# Add the standard library and build the image onto a static basekit.
# (mkimg REPLACES the basekit's own attached archive, so the stdlib must be
#  carried in the VFS tree -- see Notes.)
buildImage -out myapp -vfs app.vfs -basekit basekit-tcl -stdlib cli
.CE
.PP
For the full application builder (dependency probing, GUI bootstrap, cross\-platform), see the \fBbuild\-app\fR app and \fBdocs/guide/build\-app.md\fR.
.SS Notes
.PP
\fBzipfs mkimg\fR replaces the ZIP archive attached to the basekit. A BAWT or magicsplat basekit ships its own standard library inside that archive (mounted at \fB//zipfs:/app/tcl_library\fR); an image built with only the application would lose the stdlib and fail to start. Always put \fBtcl_library\fR (and \fBtk_library\fR for Tk apps) into the VFS tree first — \fBcopyStdlib\fR does this.
.PP
\fBzipfs\fR is part of Tcl 9. For Tcl 8.6 or for byte\-level ZIP creation and ODF container control, use \fBtclutils::tuzip\fR instead.
